window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"Observability Field Guide","section":"From staring at dashboards to understanding your systems.","tool":"home","text":"From staring at dashboards to understanding your systems. You already ship code that runs in production. This guide turns \"I watch some Grafana panels\" into the deep mental models interviewers probe - logs, metrics, traces, OpenTelemetry, Prometheus, SLOs - explained with an analogy first, then the ","keywords":["from","staring","at","dashboards","to","understanding","your","systems"]},{"url":"index.html#journey","page":"Observability Field Guide","section":"The journey: can t-debug-it -> on-call lead","tool":"home","text":"01 The journey: can t-debug-it -> on-call lead You don t level up by memorizing tools - you level up by understanding what each signal answers and what it costs . Find your level, then close the gaps. Every chip is one page. In plain English analogy Becoming a doctor for your systems. A junior reads","keywords":["the","journey","can","debug","it","on","call","lead"]},{"url":"index.html#model","page":"Observability Field Guide","section":"The one mental model: three signals, one pipeline","tool":"home","text":"02 The one mental model: three signals, one pipeline Almost everything in observability hangs off one picture - telemetry flowing from your code to a place you can ask it questions. Keep this in your head; every topic is one box on it. instrument -> collect -> store -> see 1 · instrument OpenTelemet","keywords":["the","one","mental","model","three","signals","pipeline"]},{"url":"index.html#progress","page":"Observability Field Guide","section":"Your progress","tool":"home","text":"03 Your progress Mark lessons as learned as you go - it s saved in your browser only (nothing leaves your machine). 0 of 0 lessons complete Reset progress","keywords":["your","progress"]},{"url":"index.html#inside","page":"Observability Field Guide","section":"What s inside","tool":"home","text":"04 What s inside Seven focused modules plus an interview hub. Each concept page runs plain English -> precise inside it: analogy, a visual you can poke at, multiple examples, and interview Q&A. Foundations See your systems Monitoring vs observability, the three pillars, cardinality & cost, the 2026 ","keywords":["what","inside"]},{"url":"index.html#how","page":"Observability Field Guide","section":"How to use this guide","tool":"home","text":"05 How to use this guide 1 Walk the roadmap Find your level above and click into the gaps you want to close first. 2 Take the pillar pages Each runs plain-English -> precise inside one page - analogy, a visual you can poke, examples, gotchas. 3 Answer as you go Hit the inline quizzes and \"say it out","keywords":["how","to","use","this","guide"]},{"url":"foundations/index.html","page":"Foundations — see your systems","section":"Foundations — see your systems.","tool":"foundations","text":"Foundations — see your systems. Before you reach for Prometheus or Datadog, you need the mental models: what observability actually means, what the three pillars do, why cardinality will blow your bill, and how the 2026 OSS stack fits together.","keywords":["foundations","see","your","systems"]},{"url":"foundations/index.html#map","page":"Foundations — see your systems","section":"What's in this module","tool":"foundations","text":"01 What's in this module Four pages, reading order below. Start with What is observability? for the core mental model, then work through the pillars, cardinality, and the full vendor landscape. Monitoring vs observability Known-unknowns vs unknown-unknowns. The test every interviewer uses. The three","keywords":["what","in","this","module"]},{"url":"foundations/what-is-observability.html","page":"Monitoring vs observability","section":"Monitoring vs observability.","tool":"foundations","text":"Monitoring vs observability. Monitoring watches known dashboards for known failure modes. Observability lets you ask brand-new questions of a running system — from its outputs alone — to explain behavior you've never seen before, without shipping new code.","keywords":["monitoring","vs","observability"]},{"url":"foundations/what-is-observability.html#idea","page":"Monitoring vs observability","section":"The core distinction","tool":"foundations","text":"01 The core distinction Monitoring = alerting on known-unknowns. Observability = the ability to answer unknown-unknowns from output data alone. The term comes from control theory: a system is \"observable\" if you can fully infer its internal state from its external outputs. Applied to software: if yo","keywords":["the","core","distinction"]},{"url":"foundations/what-is-observability.html#the-test","page":"Monitoring vs observability","section":"The \"can you debug it?\" test","tool":"foundations","text":"02 The \"can you debug it?\" test The sharpest test: can you debug a brand-new production problem without deploying new code or logging? If you have to add a log line, redeploy, and wait — your system is not observable. Observability means the telemetry you already ship is rich enough to answer questi","keywords":["the","can","you","debug","it","test"]},{"url":"foundations/what-is-observability.html#how-it-works","page":"Monitoring vs observability","section":"How systems become observable","tool":"foundations","text":"03 How systems become observable Observability comes from the richness of your telemetry, not the number of dashboards. Three properties matter: structured (machine-parseable), correlated (logs linked to traces, traces to metrics), and high-cardinality (enough attributes to slice by any dimension). ","keywords":["how","systems","become","observable","trace_id"]},{"url":"foundations/what-is-observability.html#interview","page":"Monitoring vs observability","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire What's the difference between monitoring and observability? core Model answer Monitoring alerts on known failure modes — you define the condition upfront. Observability is a property of the system: the ability to infer internal state from external outputs and answer novel que","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"foundations/three-pillars.html","page":"The three pillars + signals","section":"The three pillars + signals.","tool":"foundations","text":"The three pillars + signals. Logs, metrics, and traces each answer a different class of question at a different cost. Knowing which pillar to reach for — and when they overlap — is the first mental model every observability interview probes.","keywords":["the","three","pillars","signals"]},{"url":"foundations/three-pillars.html#idea","page":"The three pillars + signals","section":"Three tools, three questions","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Three tools, three questions Logs = narrative events. Metrics = cheap aggregatable numbers. Traces = causal path across services. Each pillar answers a different first question: What happened? (logs) → How many / how fast? (metrics) → Why did this request take so long? (traces). A mature system e","keywords":["three","tools","questions","trace_id"]},{"url":"foundations/three-pillars.html#pillars-compared","page":"The three pillars + signals","section":"Pillars compared","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Pillars compared Each pillar has a different cost driver and a different primary question it answers best. Use the table as a quick decision framework. Pillar Answers Cost driver Example question Best tool Logs What happened & when? Volume × retention \"What was in the request body when the error ","keywords":["pillars","compared"]},{"url":"foundations/three-pillars.html#when-to-use","page":"The three pillars + signals","section":"When to reach for each","tool":"foundations","text":"03 When to reach for each Reach for logs when… You need the raw narrative: payloads, error messages, audit trails. Expensive for high-throughput paths — sample or use structured events. Reach for metrics when… You need aggregates over time: rates, percentiles, counts. Cheap per data point but scale ","keywords":["when","to","reach","for","each"]},{"url":"foundations/three-pillars.html#emerging","page":"The three pillars + signals","section":"Emerging signals: beyond the three","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Emerging signals: beyond the three Two newer signal types are becoming mainstream: wide structured events and Profiles (continuous profiling). Wide structured events A single JSON event with 100+ attributes per request — no loss of cardinality, no pre-aggregation. Popularized by Honeycomb. OTel s","keywords":["emerging","signals","beyond","the","three"]},{"url":"foundations/three-pillars.html#interview","page":"The three pillars + signals","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Walk me through logs vs metrics vs traces — what does each answer? core Model answer Logs are discrete narrative events — rich detail, high cost at volume. Metrics are cheap aggregatable numbers over time — great for dashboards and alerts, but limited to pre-defined dimension","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"foundations/cardinality.html","page":"Cardinality & the cost model","section":"Cardinality & the cost model.","tool":"foundations","text":"Cardinality & the cost model. Cardinality is the number of unique label-value combinations your metrics system must store. It's the single biggest surprise on a Prometheus or Datadog bill — and a very common reason Prometheus OOMs in production.","keywords":["cardinality","the","cost","model"]},{"url":"foundations/cardinality.html#idea","page":"Cardinality & the cost model","section":"What cardinality means","tool":"foundations","text":"01 What cardinality means Each unique combination of label values creates one time series . Metric storage scales with the count of those series — not with request volume. A metric http_requests_total with labels method (3 values) × status (5 values) × region (4 values) = 60 series . Add user_id wit","keywords":["what","cardinality","means","http_requests_total","method","status","region","user_id","GET","200"]},{"url":"foundations/cardinality.html#cost-model","page":"Cardinality & the cost model","section":"Cost model by signal type","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Cost model by signal type Each signal has a different cost driver. Knowing this tells you where to put detail — and where not to. Metrics cost ≈ cardinality (unique label combos). Adding one unbounded label multiplies every existing series count. Logs cost ≈ volume × retention. 10 GB/day × 30 day","keywords":["cost","model","by","signal","type"]},{"url":"foundations/cardinality.html#high-cardinality-trap","page":"Cardinality & the cost model","section":"The high-cardinality trap","tool":"foundations","text":"03 The high-cardinality trap The most common production incident with Prometheus: someone adds user_id , email , or a full URL path as a metric label, then wonders why Prometheus OOMs overnight. Anti-pattern — explodes cardinality app.py Copy # ❌ user_id is unbounded — one series per user requests_t","keywords":["the","high","cardinality","trap","user_id","email","is","unbounded","one","series","per","user","requests_total","Counter","http_requests_total","method","status","DANGER","labels","GET","200","current_user","id","millions","of","values","inc","metric","low","only","detail","goes","in","trace","span","attribute","set_attribute","request_id"]},{"url":"foundations/cardinality.html#quiz-section","page":"Cardinality & the cost model","section":"Check your model","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Check your model Your team doubles traffic from 10 k req/s to 20 k req/s. Your Prometheus memory usage… Roughly doubles — more requests = more series. Stays roughly the same — series count = unique label combos, not request rate. Halves — Prometheus compresses better at higher volume. Right. Prom","keywords":["check","your","model"]},{"url":"foundations/cardinality.html#interview","page":"Cardinality & the cost model","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What is cardinality in metrics, and why does it matter? core Model answer Cardinality is the count of unique label-value combinations — i.e., the number of time series stored. Metrics storage and memory scale with cardinality, not with request volume. One unbounded label (lik","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","user_id","tsdb","status"]},{"url":"foundations/the-stack.html","page":"The 2026 stack: open source + vendors","section":"The 2026 stack: open source + vendors.","tool":"foundations","text":"The 2026 stack: open source + vendors. The OSS observability stack has standardized around OTel for instrumentation and a Prometheus/Loki/Tempo/Grafana core for storage and visualization. Understanding how each piece fits — and where commercial platforms play — is table-stakes for any senior enginee","keywords":["the","stack","open","source","vendors"]},{"url":"foundations/the-stack.html#oss-stack","page":"The 2026 stack: open source + vendors","section":"The OSS core","tool":"foundations","text":"01 The OSS core Think of it as a pipeline: OTel instruments, the Collector routes, specialized backends store, Grafana visualizes, Alertmanager pages you. Each component is replaceable. That's the power: you can swap Tempo for Jaeger v2, or ship directly to Datadog, without changing your application","keywords":["the","oss","core","trace_id"]},{"url":"foundations/the-stack.html#vendors","page":"The 2026 stack: open source + vendors","section":"Commercial platforms","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Commercial platforms Every major vendor now supports OTLP ingest. The decision is really about operations burden vs. control vs. cost at scale. Vendor Model Strength Choose when… Datadog All-in-one SaaS; host + APM pricing Polished UX, deep APM, built-in anomaly detection Team wants zero infra op","keywords":["commercial","platforms"]},{"url":"foundations/the-stack.html#trends","page":"The 2026 stack: open source + vendors","section":"2026 trends to know","tool":"foundations","text":"03 2026 trends to know OTel decouples instrumentation from backend You instrument once with OTel; route OTLP to any backend. Switching vendors no longer means re-instrumenting code. Wide structured events gaining ground One rich event per request (Honeycomb model) rather than separate logs/metrics/t","keywords":["trends","to","know"]},{"url":"foundations/the-stack.html#interview","page":"The 2026 stack: open source + vendors","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire Walk me through the 2026 OSS observability stack. core Model answer OTel SDK instruments services and emits OTLP. The OTel Collector (or Grafana Alloy) receives, processes, and routes to backends: Prometheus for metrics, Loki for logs, Tempo or Jaeger v2 for traces, Pyroscope","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"logs/index.html","page":"Logs — the narrative signal","section":"Logs - the narrative signal.","tool":"logs","text":"Logs - the narrative signal. Logs are the timestamped story of what your system did and why. They answer questions metrics can t: which user, which order, what decision was made.","keywords":["logs","the","narrative","signal"]},{"url":"logs/index.html#map","page":"Logs — the narrative signal","section":"What s in this module","tool":"logs","text":"01 What s in this module Three pages, in reading order: structure your logs so they re queryable, thread a correlation ID so they re joinable, then ship them through Loki so they re affordable. Structured logging JSON over free-text, log levels, what to log (and never log). Correlation IDs & context","keywords":["what","in","this","module"]},{"url":"logs/structured-logging.html","page":"Structured logging","section":"Structured logging.","tool":"logs","text":"Structured logging. A log line you can t query is just noise. Structured logs are machine-readable records with named fields - you can filter, aggregate, and join them the same way you query a database.","keywords":["structured","logging"]},{"url":"logs/structured-logging.html#idea","page":"Structured logging","section":"Structured vs unstructured","tool":"logs","text":"01 Structured vs unstructured Unstructured logs are prose; structured logs are records. The difference is queryability at scale. A free-text log requires regex to extract facts. A JSON log has named keys - any log backend can filter on order_id , count by user_id , or group by status instantly. In p","keywords":["structured","vs","unstructured","order_id","user_id","status","INFO","Order","placed","by","user","for","payment","OK","ts","T12","level","event","order","amount_usd","payment_status","ok"]},{"url":"logs/structured-logging.html#levels","page":"Structured logging","section":"Log levels & when to use them","tool":"logs","text":"02 Log levels & when to use them A log level is a severity signal - it tells operators which lines deserve attention and lets them mute the rest. Choose the level by answering: \"Does an operator need to act on this right now?\" Flooding ERROR with noise trains people to ignore it. DEBUG Diagnostic de","keywords":["log","levels","when","to","use","them"]},{"url":"logs/structured-logging.html#what-to-log","page":"Structured logging","section":"What to log - and what never to log","tool":"logs","text":"03 What to log - and what never to log Log decisions, IDs, and outcomes . Never log secrets, PII, or raw payloads . Good log fields help you answer \"what happened to this request?\" without needing to reproduce it. Do log request_id , user_id (opaque), order_id , HTTP status, duration, decision made,","keywords":["what","to","log","and","never","request_id","user_id","order_id","import","logging","structlog","get_logger","def","place_order","str","amount","float","None","Good","structured","fields","no","PII","payload","info","order","placed","amount_usd","Bad","do","this","Order","for","user","email","card","number"]},{"url":"logs/structured-logging.html#interview","page":"Structured logging","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"logs","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire Why use structured logging instead of print statements? warm Model answer Structured logs have named fields that log backends can index and query. You can filter level=ERROR , count by service , or join on order_id without regex. Free-text strings require parsing at query tim","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","level","ERROR","service","order_id"]},{"url":"logs/correlation.html","page":"Correlation IDs & context","section":"Correlation IDs & context.","tool":"logs","text":"Correlation IDs & context. A log line in isolation tells you nothing about the request it belongs to. A correlation ID makes every log line part of a searchable, joinable story - and lets you jump straight from a log to the trace that explains the latency.","keywords":["correlation","ids","context"]},{"url":"logs/correlation.html#idea","page":"Correlation IDs & context","section":"The idea","tool":"logs","text":"01 The idea A correlation ID (or request ID) is a single identifier generated at the edge and forwarded in every log, every downstream call, and every response header. Without it, logs from five services handling the same request look like five unrelated events. With it, one query - request_id=\"abc-","keywords":["the","idea","request_id","abc","123","Request","ID","trace_id","span_id"]},{"url":"logs/correlation.html#flow","page":"Correlation IDs & context","section":"One request, stitched across services","tool":"logs","text":"02 One request, stitched across services Every service adds its own log lines; the shared trace_id is the thread that ties them all together in your log backend. POST /checkout - trace_id: f3a7…c1e2 gateway Request received trace_id=f3a7…c1e2 span_id=aa01 status=200 orders-svc Order created trace_id","keywords":["one","request","stitched","across","services","trace_id","f3a7","c1e2"]},{"url":"logs/correlation.html#otel-context","page":"Correlation IDs & context","section":"OTel auto-injects trace context into logs","tool":"logs","text":"03 OTel auto-injects trace context into logs With OpenTelemetry, you don t manually thread IDs - the SDK injects trace_id and span_id into every log record automatically. Configure a log bridge (e.g., opentelemetry-instrumentation-logging ) and every logging.info() call gains trace context from the ","keywords":["otel","auto","injects","trace","context","into","logs","trace_id","span_id","opentelemetry","instrumentation","logging","info","from","import","structlog","log","get_logger","def","charge_card","order_id","str","amount","float","None","span","get_current_span","ctx","get_span_context","payment","attempted","amount_usd","format","With","OTel","bridge","active","the","two","lines"]},{"url":"logs/correlation.html#interview","page":"Correlation IDs & context","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"logs","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire What is a correlation ID and why does every log line need one? warm Model answer A correlation ID (or request ID) is a unique token generated when a request enters the system, forwarded in every outbound call, and stamped on every log line. Without it, logs from different ser","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","trace_id","span_id","tenant_id","user_id","session_id","Baggage"]},{"url":"logs/loki-and-pipelines.html","page":"Loki & log pipelines","section":"Loki & log pipelines.","tool":"logs","text":"Loki & log pipelines. Loki stores logs cheaply by indexing only a small set of labels and compressing everything else as raw chunks. You query the body with LogQL - fast, affordable, and natively integrated with Grafana s metrics and traces.","keywords":["loki","log","pipelines"]},{"url":"logs/loki-and-pipelines.html#model","page":"Loki & log pipelines","section":"The Loki storage model","tool":"logs","text":"01 The Loki storage model Loki indexes labels (a handful), stores log lines as compressed chunks , and searches content at query time with LogQL . Traditional log backends full-text-index every word. That s powerful but expensive. Loki trades index size for storage cost: label queries are fast; cont","keywords":["the","loki","storage","model","app","env","region","checkout","prod"]},{"url":"logs/loki-and-pipelines.html#logql","page":"Loki & log pipelines","section":"LogQL in one minute","tool":"logs","text":"02 LogQL in one minute Every LogQL query starts with a stream selector in {} , then pipes the body through filters and parsers. The two-stage structure mirrors Prometheus: first narrow to the right streams (cheap, index-based), then process the body (content scan). logql-examples Copy # All logs fro","keywords":["logql","in","one","minute","All","logs","from","checkout","that","contain","error","app","Parse","JSON","body","filter","by","status","field","json","500","Count","errors","per","metric","query","sum","rate"]},{"url":"logs/loki-and-pipelines.html#pipeline","page":"Loki & log pipelines","section":"The shipping pipeline","tool":"logs","text":"03 The shipping pipeline Log lines flow: app → Grafana Alloy → Loki → Grafana . Alloy is the recommended OTel-collector-based agent (replacing Promtail). Log shipping pipeline application Emit logs stdout / file structured JSON Grafana Alloy Collect & label tail files / OTLP in attach labels batch &","keywords":["the","shipping","pipeline","user_id","request_id","trace_id","app","env","region","json"]},{"url":"logs/loki-and-pipelines.html#cost-and-vendors","page":"Loki & log pipelines","section":"Cost control & vendor landscape","tool":"logs","text":"04 Cost control & vendor landscape Cost = ingestion volume × retention . Control both, and avoid high-cardinality label explosion (the same trap as metrics). Retention Set per-stream or globally. Hot/warm/cold tiers: recent data on fast storage, old data on cheap object storage. Drop noisy DEBUG str","keywords":["cost","control","vendor","landscape","user_id","json"]},{"url":"logs/loki-and-pipelines.html#interview","page":"Loki & log pipelines","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"logs","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire How does Loki differ from Elasticsearch for log storage? warm Model answer Loki indexes only the label set, not the log body. This makes storage very cheap (compressed chunks in object storage) but shifts search cost to query time. Elasticsearch full-text-indexes every token,","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","user_id","request_id","json"]},{"url":"metrics/index.html","page":"Metrics — the cheap signal","section":"Metrics — the cheap signal.","tool":"metrics","text":"Metrics — the cheap signal. Metrics are numbers sampled over time — the lightest, cheapest observability signal. Master the four types, two diagnostic frameworks, and the math behind percentiles and you'll answer every \"is the system healthy?\" question with confidence.","keywords":["metrics","the","cheap","signal"]},{"url":"metrics/index.html#map","page":"Metrics — the cheap signal","section":"What's in this module","tool":"metrics","text":"01 What's in this module Three pages, in reading order: understand the building blocks first, then the frameworks that tell you which metrics to watch, then the math that makes those metrics trustworthy. The four metric types Counter, gauge, histogram, summary — when to use each and the gotchas that","keywords":["what","in","this","module"]},{"url":"metrics/metric-types.html","page":"The four metric types","section":"The four metric types.","tool":"metrics","text":"The four metric types. Every Prometheus-style metric is one of four shapes. Choose the wrong shape and your graphs are meaningless — or worse, silently misleading.","keywords":["the","four","metric","types"]},{"url":"metrics/metric-types.html#idea","page":"The four metric types","section":"The four shapes","tool":"metrics","text":"01 The four shapes Pick the type that matches how the value behaves over time — not what it measures. Each type encodes different math. A counter reset looks catastrophic on a gauge graph. A summary's quantiles can't be added across instances. Getting this right is foundational. In plain English ana","keywords":["the","four","shapes"]},{"url":"metrics/metric-types.html#types-detail","page":"The four metric types","section":"Type-by-type reference","tool":"metrics","text":"02 Type-by-type reference Type Behaviour How to query Best for Counter Monotonic, only goes up; resets to 0 on restart rate() or increase() Requests, errors, bytes sent Gauge Snapshot — can go up or down freely Raw value; or avg_over_time() Memory, queue depth, concurrency, temp Histogram Buckets of","keywords":["type","by","reference","rate","increase","avg_over_time","histogram_quantile","_bucket","_quantile","005","enable","feature","native","histograms"]},{"url":"metrics/metric-types.html#when-to-use","page":"The four metric types","section":"Choosing the right type","tool":"metrics","text":"03 Choosing the right type 1 Does the value only go up? Yes → Counter . Examples: http_requests_total , errors_total , bytes_sent_total . 2 Is it a snapshot of current state? Yes → Gauge . Examples: memory_usage_bytes , active_connections , queue depth. 3 Do you need percentiles across multiple inst","keywords":["choosing","the","right","type","http_requests_total","errors_total","bytes_sent_total","memory_usage_bytes","active_connections","rate","_quantile"]},{"url":"metrics/metric-types.html#quiz-types","page":"The four metric types","section":"Check yourself","tool":"metrics","text":"04 Check yourself You want to track the number of cache hits since the service started. Which type? Counter — it only ever increases. Gauge — it tracks a current count. Summary — you'll want percentiles later. Right. Cache hits accumulate monotonically. Use rate(cache_hits_total[5m]) to get hits per","keywords":["check","yourself","rate","cache_hits_total","p99","sum","avg","histogram_quantile"]},{"url":"metrics/metric-types.html#interview","page":"The four metric types","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"metrics","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What's the difference between a counter and a gauge? warm Model answer A counter is monotonically increasing — it only goes up and resets on restart. A gauge is a snapshot of current state that can go up or down freely. You should always derive a rate from a counter; you can ","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","histogram_quantile","enable","feature","native","histograms"]},{"url":"metrics/red-use.html","page":"RED & USE methods","section":"RED & USE methods.","tool":"metrics","text":"RED & USE methods. Two complementary checklists tell you exactly which metrics to look at first. RED watches your services from the user's perspective; USE watches your infrastructure from the inside. Together they map to Google's Four Golden Signals.","keywords":["red","use","methods"]},{"url":"metrics/red-use.html#idea","page":"RED & USE methods","section":"The mental model","tool":"metrics","text":"01 The mental model RED = \"is the service doing its job?\" USE = \"is the resource holding up?\" Neither framework replaces the other. A database can be saturated (USE) before any user-facing error appears (RED). Conversely, high error rates in RED rarely show up in CPU utilisation. In plain English an","keywords":["the","mental","model"]},{"url":"metrics/red-use.html#red","page":"RED & USE methods","section":"RED — for request-driven services","tool":"metrics","text":"02 RED — for request-driven services Coined by Tom Wilkie (Grafana Labs). Apply it to every microservice or API endpoint. RED checklist R Rate Requests/sec arriving at this service E Errors Fraction of requests returning an error D Duration Latency distribution (p50 / p95 / p99) Start here for every","keywords":["red","for","request","driven","services","rate","http_requests_total","status","histogram_quantile","http_request_duration_seconds_bucket"]},{"url":"metrics/red-use.html#use","page":"RED & USE methods","section":"USE — for resource health","tool":"metrics","text":"03 USE — for resource health Coined by Brendan Gregg. Apply it to every resource: CPU, memory, disk I/O, network, connection pools, queues. Letter Meaning Example metric U tilization % of time the resource is busy (0–100%) CPU usage %, disk busy % S aturation Extra work queued that the resource can'","keywords":["use","for","resource","health"]},{"url":"metrics/red-use.html#golden-signals","page":"RED & USE methods","section":"Four Golden Signals — the overlap","tool":"metrics","text":"04 Four Golden Signals — the overlap Google's SRE book defined four signals: Latency , Traffic , Errors , Saturation . They overlap heavily with RED and USE. Golden Signal Maps to RED Maps to USE Latency Duration (D) — Traffic Rate (R) Utilization (U) Errors Errors (E) Errors (E) Saturation — Satura","keywords":["four","golden","signals","the","overlap"]},{"url":"metrics/red-use.html#quiz-red-use","page":"RED & USE methods","section":"Check yourself","tool":"metrics","text":"05 Check yourself Your checkout service returns 500s for 3% of requests. Which letter in RED does this map to? E — Errors: the fraction of requests that failed. D — Duration: high error rates slow things down. Both R and E — rate includes errors. Right. Errors in RED is specifically the error ratio.","keywords":["check","yourself"]},{"url":"metrics/red-use.html#interview","page":"RED & USE methods","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"metrics","text":"06 Interview rapid-fire Explain RED and USE and when you'd use each. core Model answer RED (Rate, Errors, Duration) applies to every request-driven microservice — it tells you how well the service is serving users. USE (Utilization, Saturation, Errors) applies to every underlying resource — CPU, mem","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"metrics/histograms-percentiles.html","page":"Histograms & percentiles","section":"Histograms & percentiles.","tool":"metrics","text":"Histograms & percentiles. The average lies. A handful of slow requests get drowned in thousands of fast ones — they stay invisible until your most valuable users start churning. Percentiles expose them. But percentiles have their own trap: you cannot average them across instances.","keywords":["histograms","percentiles"]},{"url":"metrics/histograms-percentiles.html#idea","page":"Histograms & percentiles","section":"Why averages lie","tool":"metrics","text":"01 Why averages lie The mean hides the tail . A p99 shows you the worst experience 1 in 100 users actually had. If 990 requests complete in 10 ms and 10 requests take 5 000 ms, the average is ~59 ms — a number that describes nobody's real experience. The p99 is 5 000 ms, which is the truth the avera","keywords":["why","averages","lie"]},{"url":"metrics/histograms-percentiles.html#histogram-mechanics","page":"Histograms & percentiles","section":"How histograms work","tool":"metrics","text":"02 How histograms work A Prometheus histogram records every observation into pre-defined buckets (cumulative upper bounds). Each scrape exposes three series: _bucket , _count , _sum . Percentiles are computed server-side from the bucket data. Latency buckets + tail-weight slider A bar chart of reque","keywords":["how","histograms","work","_bucket","_count","_sum","p99","across","ALL","pods","valid","because","we","aggregate","buckets","first","histogram_quantile","sum","by","le","rate","http_request_duration_seconds_bucket"]},{"url":"metrics/histograms-percentiles.html#buckets","page":"Histograms & percentiles","section":"Bucket boundaries & native histograms","tool":"metrics","text":"03 Bucket boundaries & native histograms Classic histograms require you to declare bucket boundaries at instrumentation time. Choose too few and you lose resolution at the tail; too many and cardinality explodes. Classic histogram Fixed buckets declared upfront: [0.005, 0.01, 0.05, 0.1, 0.5, 1, +Inf","keywords":["bucket","boundaries","native","histograms","005","Inf","enable","feature","sum","by","le"]},{"url":"metrics/histograms-percentiles.html#quiz-hist","page":"Histograms & percentiles","section":"Check yourself","tool":"metrics","text":"04 Check yourself Your service has 5 pods. Each reports a Prometheus summary p99 of roughly 200 ms. What is the system-wide p99? Unknown — summary quantiles can't be aggregated; you need a histogram to get a valid cross-pod p99. 200 ms — the pods are identical so the average is correct. 1000 ms — yo","keywords":["check","yourself","histogram_quantile","sum","by","le","rate","_bucket"]},{"url":"metrics/histograms-percentiles.html#interview","page":"Histograms & percentiles","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"metrics","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Why do we track p99 instead of average latency? warm Model answer The average hides the tail. A small number of very slow requests get diluted by the majority of fast ones, making the system look healthy while a fraction of users — often the most active — experience unaccepta","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","sum","by","le","histogram_quantile","Inf","enable","feature","native","histograms"]},{"url":"traces/index.html","page":"Traces — the causal signal","section":"Traces — the causal signal.","tool":"traces","text":"Traces — the causal signal. Metrics tell you something is wrong ; logs tell you what happened on one machine . Only traces answer the question that matters most: where did the time go across services?","keywords":["traces","the","causal","signal"]},{"url":"traces/index.html#map","page":"Traces — the causal signal","section":"What's in this module","tool":"traces","text":"01 What's in this module Three pages, reading order below. Start with Spans & context propagation for the vocabulary, then learn to read the critical path, then tackle sampling trade-offs. Spans & context propagation What a span is, how traces stitch together across processes via the W3C traceparent","keywords":["what","in","this","module"]},{"url":"traces/spans-and-context.html","page":"Spans & context propagation","section":"Spans & context propagation.","tool":"traces","text":"Spans & context propagation. A trace is a tree of spans — timed checkpoints that follow a request across every service it touches. The magic glue that stitches them together is context propagation : a tiny header passed with every outbound call.","keywords":["spans","context","propagation"]},{"url":"traces/spans-and-context.html#span-anatomy","page":"Spans & context propagation","section":"Anatomy of a span","tool":"traces","text":"01 Anatomy of a span A span = one timed operation with a name, start/end timestamps, attributes, and a status code. Every span carries a trace_id (shared across the whole request) and a span_id (unique to this operation). A parent_span_id links child to parent, building the tree. In plain English an","keywords":["anatomy","of","span","trace_id","span_id","parent_span_id","http","method","db","statement","user_id","OK","ERROR","UNSET"]},{"url":"traces/spans-and-context.html#trace-waterfall","page":"Spans & context propagation","section":"A trace in practice","tool":"traces","text":"02 A trace in practice A trace = the full tree of spans sharing one trace_id . The waterfall below shows a GET /search request fanning out from an API gateway into a search service, which hits a database and a Redis cache in parallel. GET /search — 210 ms end-to-end GET /search: gateway → search-svc","keywords":["trace","in","practice","trace_id","GET","search"]},{"url":"traces/spans-and-context.html#propagation","page":"Spans & context propagation","section":"Context propagation across services","tool":"traces","text":"03 Context propagation across services When a service makes an outbound HTTP call, the OTel SDK injects the current trace context as a traceparent header (W3C Trace Context standard). The downstream service extracts it and creates a child span — linking its work into the same trace tree. W3C tracepa","keywords":["context","propagation","across","services","traceparent","bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736","f067aa0ba902b7","version","trace_id","128","bit","hex","parent_span_id","flags","tracestate","span_id"]},{"url":"traces/spans-and-context.html#otel-kinds","page":"Spans & context propagation","section":"Span kinds & semantic conventions","tool":"traces","text":"04 Span kinds & semantic conventions OTel defines span kinds to model the role of each operation. Backends use these for service graph topology and latency attribution. Kind What it represents Common attributes SERVER Inbound RPC/HTTP handler http.method , http.route , http.status_code CLIENT Outbou","keywords":["span","kinds","semantic","conventions","SERVER","http","method","route","status_code","CLIENT","server","address","url","full","PRODUCER","messaging","system","destination","CONSUMER","operation","INTERNAL","OTEL_SEMCONV_STABILITY_OPT_IN"]},{"url":"traces/spans-and-context.html#interview","page":"Spans & context propagation","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"traces","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What's the difference between a span and a trace? warm Model answer A span is a single timed operation with a name, timestamps, and attributes. A trace is the full tree of spans that share one trace_id — it represents a complete request end-to-end, stitching together work fro","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","trace_id","span_id","traceparent","tracestate"]},{"url":"traces/critical-path.html","page":"Reading a trace: the critical path","section":"Reading a trace: the critical path.","tool":"traces","text":"Reading a trace: the critical path. A trace waterfall is more than a pretty picture — it's a latency budget broken into named segments. Knowing how to find the critical path is the skill that turns a 2-second trace into a one-line root cause.","keywords":["reading","trace","the","critical","path"]},{"url":"traces/critical-path.html#critical-path-concept","page":"Reading a trace: the critical path","section":"What is the critical path?","tool":"traces","text":"01 What is the critical path? The critical path is the chain of spans that directly determines end-to-end latency — shortening anything off this chain has zero effect on the user. Spans run serially or in parallel. Parallel spans overlap in time; only the longest sibling matters. The critical path =","keywords":["what","is","the","critical","path"]},{"url":"traces/critical-path.html#waterfall-reading","page":"Reading a trace: the critical path","section":"Reading the waterfall","tool":"traces","text":"02 Reading the waterfall The trace below shows a checkout request. The Redis cache read and the inventory gRPC call run in parallel — but inventory takes longer, so it owns the critical path. The payment call is strictly serial after inventory completes. POST /checkout — 420 ms end-to-end POST /chec","keywords":["reading","the","waterfall"]},{"url":"traces/critical-path.html#n-plus-one","page":"Reading a trace: the critical path","section":"The N+1 pattern","tool":"traces","text":"03 The N+1 pattern A dead giveaway in traces: a parent span spawning N identical short sequential spans — usually DB queries in a loop. The loop is invisible in metrics (aggregate latency looks reasonable) but jumps off a trace waterfall. GET /orders — N+1 query (6 items) GET /orders: one SELECT for","keywords":["the","pattern","orders","db","query","SELECT","id","FROM","for","in","round","trips","items","WHERE","order_id","ids","Single","trip","ANY"]},{"url":"traces/critical-path.html#tail-latency","page":"Reading a trace: the critical path","section":"Tail latency & fan-out","tool":"traces","text":"04 Tail latency & fan-out When a service fans out to N downstream calls in parallel and waits for all of them, the response time = max(all N) , not the average. One slow dependency drives every request's tail latency. Fan-out: slowest shard sets the pace request search-svc fans out to 4 shards paral","keywords":["tail","latency","fan","out"]},{"url":"traces/critical-path.html#interview","page":"Reading a trace: the critical path","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"traces","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire How do you find the root cause of a latency spike using a trace? core Model answer Find the critical path — the chain of spans from root to leaf with no parallel shortcut. Whichever span on that chain is longest is your bottleneck. Look at its attributes: is it a slow query, ","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"traces/sampling.html","page":"Trace sampling","section":"Trace sampling.","tool":"traces","text":"Trace sampling. A high-traffic service can produce millions of traces per minute. You can't store them all — and most are boring successes. Sampling decides which traces to keep, but the wrong strategy throws away the exact traces you needed to debug.","keywords":["trace","sampling"]},{"url":"traces/sampling.html#why-sample","page":"Trace sampling","section":"Why sampling exists","tool":"traces","text":"01 Why sampling exists Cost at scale : a 10 k-RPS service at 5 spans/request = 50,000 spans/sec. At ~1 KB each, that's ~4 TB/day — before replication. Sampling reduces volume while preserving signal. The art is keeping the traces that matter — slow requests, errors, novel paths — without requiring y","keywords":["why","sampling","exists"]},{"url":"traces/sampling.html#strategies","page":"Trace sampling","section":"Head vs tail vs error — the trade-offs","tool":"traces","text":"02 Head vs tail vs error — the trade-offs Strategy Decision point Knows outcome? Infra cost Risk Head (probabilistic) At trace start, in SDK No Near-zero Discards rare errors & outliers Tail After trace completes, in Collector Yes Buffer memory per active trace Complex; buffer overflow loses traces ","keywords":["head","vs","tail","error","the","trade","offs"]},{"url":"traces/sampling.html#where-sampling-happens","page":"Trace sampling","section":"Where sampling happens","tool":"traces","text":"03 Where sampling happens Sampling can live in the SDK (head only), in the OTel Collector (head or tail), or in the backend. Each stage has different visibility — later is more powerful but more expensive. 1 SDK (head only) The OTel SDK samples at span creation time. The TraceIdRatioBased sampler ke","keywords":["where","sampling","happens","TraceIdRatioBased","trace_id","tail_sampling","processors","decision_wait","buffer","window","policies","name","keep","errors","type","status_code","status_codes","ERROR","slow","latency","threshold_ms","500","probabilistic","sampling_percentage"]},{"url":"traces/sampling.html#backends","page":"Trace sampling","section":"Tracing backends in 2026","tool":"traces","text":"04 Tracing backends in 2026 The backend stores spans and provides trace search + waterfall UI. Two dominant OSS backends, plus Honeycomb's \"wide events\" angle. Backend Storage model Query language Key angle Jaeger v2 Pluggable (Cassandra, ES, BadgerDB) Tag filters + service graph Rebuilt on the OTel","keywords":["tracing","backends","in","trace_id"]},{"url":"traces/sampling.html#interview","page":"Trace sampling","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"traces","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What's the difference between head sampling and tail sampling? core Model answer Head sampling decides at the start of the request — before any outcome is known — using a random or hash-based coin flip. It's cheap but discards errors and outliers at the same rate as normal tr","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","tail_sampling"]},{"url":"otel/index.html","page":"OpenTelemetry — one standard","section":"OpenTelemetry — one standard.","tool":"otel","text":"OpenTelemetry — one standard. Before OTel, every vendor shipped its own agent, SDK, and wire format. Swapping backends meant re-instrumenting your whole codebase. OTel ended that: one vendor-neutral standard for traces, metrics, logs — and now profiles — that any backend can consume.","keywords":["opentelemetry","one","standard"]},{"url":"otel/index.html#map","page":"OpenTelemetry — one standard","section":"What's in this module","tool":"otel","text":"01 What's in this module OTel has three layers (API, SDK, Collector) and two cross-cutting concerns (instrumentation strategy + semantic conventions). Learn them in order — each page builds on the last. API, SDK & Collector The three-layer architecture and why the Collector is the pivot point of eve","keywords":["what","in","this","module"]},{"url":"otel/api-sdk-collector.html","page":"API, SDK & Collector","section":"API, SDK & Collector.","tool":"otel","text":"API, SDK & Collector. OTel splits responsibilities across three layers so you can change your backend, sampling policy, or export destination without touching application code. All three speak one protocol: OTLP .","keywords":["api","sdk","collector"]},{"url":"otel/api-sdk-collector.html#three-layers","page":"API, SDK & Collector","section":"The three layers","tool":"otel","text":"01 The three layers The API defines what to call. The SDK defines what happens when you call it. The Collector decouples your app from any backend. This separation is deliberate. A library can depend on the API (stable, no-op by default) without pulling in a concrete SDK. Ops teams swap the SDK conf","keywords":["the","three","layers"]},{"url":"otel/api-sdk-collector.html#pipeline","page":"API, SDK & Collector","section":"The full pipeline","tool":"otel","text":"02 The full pipeline In production the data flows from your app through the Collector before reaching any backend. The Collector is the swap point — change an exporter config, not application code. OTel data path — app to backends App OTel SDK Instruments code, batches spans/metrics/logs, sends via ","keywords":["the","full","pipeline"]},{"url":"otel/api-sdk-collector.html#why-collector","page":"API, SDK & Collector","section":"Why the Collector matters","tool":"otel","text":"03 Why the Collector matters Skipping the Collector and pointing your SDK directly at a vendor endpoint is the most common OTel mistake. It looks simpler — until you need to add a second backend, apply sampling, or switch vendors. Anti-pattern — direct export SDK → Vendor A endpoint. Changing backen","keywords":["why","the","collector","matters"]},{"url":"otel/api-sdk-collector.html#interview","page":"API, SDK & Collector","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"otel","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire What's the difference between the OTel API and the OTel SDK? core Model answer The API is the interface — the calls you make in your code. It's a no-op if nothing else is loaded, which makes it safe for library authors. The SDK is the concrete implementation that wires up sam","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"otel/instrumentation.html","page":"Auto vs manual instrumentation","section":"Auto vs manual instrumentation.","tool":"otel","text":"Auto vs manual instrumentation. Auto-instrumentation gives you HTTP, database, and framework spans for free. Manual instrumentation adds the business-logic context that makes those spans actually debuggable. Use both — they answer different questions.","keywords":["auto","vs","manual","instrumentation"]},{"url":"otel/instrumentation.html#two-kinds","page":"Auto vs manual instrumentation","section":"Two kinds of instrumentation","tool":"otel","text":"01 Two kinds of instrumentation Auto-instrumentation patches known libraries at load time. Manual instrumentation adds spans and attributes around your own logic. Auto-instrumentation (agents, bytecode injection, monkey-patching) works at the framework boundary. It sees requests and DB calls but kno","keywords":["two","kinds","of","instrumentation","SELECT","user_id","order_id","order","id","cart","item_count","payment","method","checkout"]},{"url":"otel/instrumentation.html#manual-example","page":"Auto vs manual instrumentation","section":"Writing a manual span","tool":"otel","text":"02 Writing a manual span The OTel Python API is three calls: get a tracer, start a span as a context manager, set attributes. The span automatically becomes a child of whatever is currently active. checkout.py Copy from opentelemetry import trace tracer = trace.get_tracer(\"orders.service\") def proce","keywords":["writing","manual","span","from","opentelemetry","import","trace","tracer","get_tracer","orders","service","def","process_checkout","cart","with","start_as_current_span","checkout","as","set_attribute","item_count","len","items","total_usd","total","user","id","user_id","auto","instrumented","DB","call","becomes","child","automatically","order","db","insert_order","return","name"]},{"url":"otel/instrumentation.html#what-to-instrument","page":"Auto vs manual instrumentation","section":"What to instrument — and what not to","tool":"otel","text":"03 What to instrument — and what not to Over-instrumenting is as harmful as under-instrumenting. Every span has a storage cost and a cardinality cost. Instrument the decisions and data that an SRE would need to answer \"why did this fail for this user?\" Common trap gotcha Adding attributes that are u","keywords":["what","to","instrument","and","not","http","status_code","payment","method","user","id","raw_sql_query","checkout","fraud","check","inventory","reserve","order","tier","full_request_json","cart","item_count"]},{"url":"otel/instrumentation.html#interview","page":"Auto vs manual instrumentation","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"otel","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire What's the difference between auto and manual instrumentation? When do you need both? core Model answer Auto-instrumentation patches known libraries at startup — you get HTTP, DB, and framework spans for free with zero code changes. Manual instrumentation is where you call th","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","start_as_current_span"]},{"url":"otel/semantic-conventions.html","page":"Semantic conventions","section":"Semantic conventions.","tool":"otel","text":"Semantic conventions. Semantic conventions are the agreed-upon names for telemetry attributes — http.request.method , db.system , service.name . They turn ad-hoc attribute names into a portable vocabulary that every dashboard, alert, and query can rely on.","keywords":["semantic","conventions"]},{"url":"otel/semantic-conventions.html#why-conventions","page":"Semantic conventions","section":"Why naming matters","tool":"otel","text":"01 Why naming matters Without conventions, one team writes method , another writes http_verb , a third writes request.method . No single dashboard works for all three. Semantic conventions fix this by standardising attribute names across every language and vendor. When your Python service and your G","keywords":["why","naming","matters","method","http_verb","request","http","service","name","version","host","k8s","pod","response","status_code","db","system","operation","gen_ai"]},{"url":"otel/semantic-conventions.html#common-attrs","page":"Semantic conventions","section":"Common attributes in practice","tool":"otel","text":"02 Common attributes in practice These are the attributes interviewers expect you to recall. They're the basis for every SLO query, every latency dashboard, every error-rate alert. service.name Resource. The logical name of your service — the primary grouping key in most backends. http.request.metho","keywords":["common","attributes","in","practice","service","name","http","request","method","GET","POST","response","status_code","url","path","user","id","db","system","postgresql","redis","mysql","operation","SELECT","INSERT","messaging","kafka","rabbitmq","sqs","gen_ai","openai","anthropic"]},{"url":"otel/semantic-conventions.html#stability-profiles","page":"Semantic conventions","section":"Stability tiers & migration","tool":"otel","text":"03 Stability tiers & migration OTel attribute groups graduate through stability tiers. Stable groups get a long-term compatibility guarantee; experimental groups can still break between versions. Group Status (~v1.41) Example attributes HTTP Stable http.request.method , http.response.status_code , u","keywords":["stability","tiers","migration","http","request","method","response","status_code","url","path","db","system","operation","name","messaging","destination","gen_ai","model","usage","input_tokens","myapp","http_method","request_verb","OTEL_SEMCONV_STABILITY_OPT_IN"]},{"url":"otel/semantic-conventions.html#interview","page":"Semantic conventions","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"otel","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire What are OTel semantic conventions and why do they matter? core Model answer Semantic conventions are standardised attribute names defined by OTel — things like http.request.method and db.system . They matter because they make telemetry portable: any dashboard, alert, or quer","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","http","request","method","db","system","gen_ai"]},{"url":"promstack/index.html","page":"Prometheus & Grafana","section":"Prometheus & Grafana.","tool":"promstack","text":"Prometheus & Grafana. The de-facto open-source duo for metrics and dashboards. Prometheus scrapes and stores time-series data; Grafana turns it into dashboards and alerts that teams actually use.","keywords":["prometheus","grafana"]},{"url":"promstack/index.html#map","page":"Prometheus & Grafana","section":"What's in this module","tool":"promstack","text":"01 What's in this module Four pages, reading order below. Start with the Prometheus model to understand the pull/scrape mental model, then PromQL for querying, then dashboards, then alerting. The Prometheus model Pull/scrape, exporters, TSDB, service discovery, Remote-Write 2.0 & OTLP ingestion. Pro","keywords":["what","in","this","module","rate","sum","by","histogram_quantile"]},{"url":"promstack/prometheus-model.html","page":"The Prometheus model","section":"The Prometheus model.","tool":"promstack","text":"The Prometheus model. Prometheus is a pull-based metrics system: it scrapes a /metrics endpoint on every target, on a schedule, and stores the results in a local time-series database. Understanding pull vs push, and the label-based data model, is the foundation for everything else.","keywords":["the","prometheus","model"]},{"url":"promstack/prometheus-model.html#pull-scrape","page":"The Prometheus model","section":"Pull / scrape model","tool":"promstack","text":"01 Pull / scrape model Prometheus reaches out to each target on a configurable interval (default 15 s) and reads its /metrics endpoint — targets don't call home. Each target exposes metrics in the Prometheus text format (or OTLP — see below). Prometheus stores the scraped samples with a timestamp an","keywords":["pull","scrape","model","metrics"]},{"url":"promstack/prometheus-model.html#exporters-sd","page":"The Prometheus model","section":"Exporters & service discovery","tool":"promstack","text":"02 Exporters & service discovery Not every service natively exposes Prometheus metrics. Exporters translate third-party systems into the text format. Service discovery lets Prometheus find targets automatically instead of hardcoding IPs. node_exporter Host-level CPU, memory, disk, network — the stan","keywords":["exporters","service","discovery"]},{"url":"promstack/prometheus-model.html#tsdb-labels","page":"The Prometheus model","section":"TSDB & the label-based data model","tool":"promstack","text":"03 TSDB & the label-based data model Every metric is identified by its name + a set of key=value labels . Each unique label combination is a separate time series stored in the local TSDB. Labels make aggregation and filtering powerful — e.g. http_requests_total{method=\"POST\", status=\"200\", service=\"","keywords":["tsdb","the","label","based","data","model","http_requests_total","method","POST","status","200","service","checkout","HELP","Total","HTTP","requests","TYPE","counter","GET","api","500","user_id","request_id"]},{"url":"promstack/prometheus-model.html#prom3-features","page":"The Prometheus model","section":"Prometheus 3.x & scaling out","tool":"promstack","text":"04 Prometheus 3.x & scaling out Prometheus 3.x (3.7 shipped Oct 2025) added three features worth knowing for interviews: native OTLP ingestion, Remote-Write 2.0, and native histograms. Native OTLP ingestion Prometheus 3.x exposes /api/v1/otlp/v1/metrics — OTel SDKs can push directly without a separa","keywords":["prometheus","scaling","out","api","v1","otlp","metrics","enable","feature","native","histograms"]},{"url":"promstack/prometheus-model.html#interview","page":"The Prometheus model","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"promstack","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Why does Prometheus use a pull model instead of push? core Model answer Pull gives Prometheus control over the scrape schedule and makes it immediately obvious when a target is down (missed scrape). It also means targets are passive — no agent needs credentials to send data. ","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","user_id","request_id"]},{"url":"promstack/promql.html","page":"PromQL, hands-on","section":"PromQL, hands-on.","tool":"promstack","text":"PromQL, hands-on. PromQL is the query language for Prometheus time-series data. Learn five patterns — selectors, rate() , aggregations, percentiles, recording rules — and you can answer almost any metrics question an interviewer throws at you.","keywords":["promql","hands","on"]},{"url":"promstack/promql.html#mental-model","page":"PromQL, hands-on","section":"The mental model","tool":"promstack","text":"01 The mental model PromQL is SQL for time series : you select a metric, filter by labels, apply functions, and aggregate. The key distinction from SQL is the time dimension — every result is a vector of current values or a range of values over a window. Two core types: an instant vector (one value ","keywords":["the","mental","model","rate","SELECT","GROUP","BY","http_requests_total","status","500","increase"]},{"url":"promstack/promql.html#selectors-rate","page":"PromQL, hands-on","section":"Selectors, matchers, and rate()","tool":"promstack","text":"02 Selectors, matchers, and rate() Label matchers filter which series you get. rate() turns a monotonically increasing counter into a per-second rate — the most useful function in PromQL. selectors.promql Copy # Exact match http_requests_total{service=\"checkout\", status=\"200\"} # Regex match — all 5x","keywords":["selectors","matchers","and","rate","Exact","match","http_requests_total","service","checkout","status","200","Regex","all","xx","errors","Negative","exclude","internal","health","checks","path","healthz","Rate","of","requests","over","minute","window","per","second","memory_bytes","avg_over_time","delta"]},{"url":"promstack/promql.html#aggregation","page":"PromQL, hands-on","section":"Aggregation with sum by","tool":"promstack","text":"03 Aggregation with sum by Raw series are per-instance. sum by (...) collapses them into meaningful aggregates — total RPS by service, error rate fleet-wide, etc. aggregation.promql Copy # Total RPS across all instances, grouped by service sum by (service) (rate(http_requests_total[5m])) # Error rat","keywords":["aggregation","with","sum","by","Total","RPS","across","all","instances","grouped","service","rate","http_requests_total","Error","ratio","errors","total","status","api"]},{"url":"promstack/promql.html#percentiles-recording","page":"PromQL, hands-on","section":"Percentiles & recording rules","tool":"promstack","text":"04 Percentiles & recording rules Latency percentiles require a histogram metric. histogram_quantile() reconstructs a percentile estimate from the bucket counts. Recording rules precompute expensive queries so dashboards load instantly. percentiles.promql Copy # p95 request latency by service histogr","keywords":["percentiles","recording","rules","histogram_quantile","p95","request","latency","by","service","sum","le","rate","http_request_duration_seconds_bucket","groups","name","slo_precompute","record","job","http_requests_total","rate5m","expr"]},{"url":"promstack/promql.html#interview","page":"PromQL, hands-on","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"promstack","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What's the difference between an instant vector and a range vector in PromQL? core Model answer An instant vector gives one current value per matching series. A range vector (with a duration selector like [5m] ) gives a set of timestamped samples over that window — it's the r","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","rate","_bucket","_count","_sum","histogram_quantile","sum","by","le"]},{"url":"promstack/grafana-dashboards.html","page":"Grafana dashboards","section":"Grafana dashboards.","tool":"promstack","text":"Grafana dashboards. A Grafana dashboard is a collection of panels wired to one or more data sources. The difference between a dashboard that's actually used and one that nobody opens comes down to structure, template variables, and discipline about what to show.","keywords":["grafana","dashboards"]},{"url":"promstack/grafana-dashboards.html#panels-datasources","page":"Grafana dashboards","section":"Panels, data sources & template variables","tool":"promstack","text":"01 Panels, data sources & template variables A panel runs one or more queries against a data source and renders the result. Template variables turn static dashboards into dynamic ones — a single dashboard covers all services with a dropdown instead of 50 clones. In plain English analogy A car dashbo","keywords":["panels","data","sources","template","variables","service"]},{"url":"promstack/grafana-dashboards.html#good-dashboard","page":"Grafana dashboards","section":"What makes a good dashboard","tool":"promstack","text":"02 What makes a good dashboard Structure determines usefulness. A wall of 50 unordered graphs is decoration. The RED method (Rate, Errors, Duration) applied per service, top-down, turns a dashboard into a diagnostic tool. Well-structured service dashboard 1 Overview row — fleet health Total RPS, err","keywords":["what","makes","good","dashboard","service"]},{"url":"promstack/grafana-dashboards.html#grafana12","page":"Grafana dashboards","section":"Grafana 12 features worth knowing","tool":"promstack","text":"03 Grafana 12 features worth knowing Two new capabilities in Grafana 12–12.3 matter for interviews: Observability-as-Code (Git Sync) and Drilldown GA. Before Git Sync Dashboard JSON lives in Grafana's DB Changes are ad-hoc, unreviewed Rollback = manual export/import No way to diff two dashboard vers","keywords":["grafana","features","worth","knowing","service"]},{"url":"promstack/grafana-dashboards.html#interview","page":"Grafana dashboards","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"promstack","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire What makes a Grafana dashboard actually useful during an incident? core Model answer Structure and signal. A useful dashboard shows RED metrics (Rate, Errors, Duration) top-down — fleet overview → per-service → per-instance — so you can narrow from \"something is wrong\" to \"th","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"promstack/alertmanager.html","page":"Alerting with Alertmanager","section":"Alerting with Alertmanager.","tool":"promstack","text":"Alerting with Alertmanager. Alert rules live in Prometheus — they evaluate a PromQL expression on a schedule. Routing, grouping, deduplication, and silencing live in Alertmanager . Keeping these two responsibilities separate is what makes the system composable and survivable.","keywords":["alerting","with","alertmanager"]},{"url":"promstack/alertmanager.html#alert-rules","page":"Alerting with Alertmanager","section":"Alert rules in Prometheus","tool":"promstack","text":"01 Alert rules in Prometheus A rule defines a PromQL expression that, when true for a sustained duration ( for: ), transitions to FIRING and Prometheus sends the alert to Alertmanager. The for: duration prevents flapping — a transient 1-second spike won't page anyone if the rule says \"must be true f","keywords":["alert","rules","in","prometheus","for","groups","name","api_alerts","HighErrorRate","expr","sum","by","service","rate","http_requests_total","status","labels","severity","page","team","backend","annotations","summary","High","error","on","description","Error","is","value","humanizePercentage"]},{"url":"promstack/alertmanager.html#alertmanager-routing","page":"Alerting with Alertmanager","section":"Alertmanager: routing & grouping","tool":"promstack","text":"02 Alertmanager: routing & grouping Alertmanager receives raw alerts from Prometheus and applies four operations before dispatching: grouping , deduplication , inhibition , and silencing . The routing tree then sends the group to the right receiver. Alert lifecycle through Alertmanager source Rule f","keywords":["alertmanager","routing","grouping","group_by","service","checkout"]},{"url":"promstack/alertmanager.html#alert-fatigue","page":"Alerting with Alertmanager","section":"Avoiding alert fatigue","tool":"promstack","text":"03 Avoiding alert fatigue Alert fatigue kills on-call. The root causes are almost always the same: too granular (per-instance instead of per-service), too many (alerting on symptoms and all their possible causes), and no for: duration. Alert fatigue anti-pattern Alert per pod: 200 pods = 200 alerts ","keywords":["avoiding","alert","fatigue","for","group_by","service"]},{"url":"promstack/alertmanager.html#interview","page":"Alerting with Alertmanager","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"promstack","text":"04 Interview rapid-fire What's the difference between an alert rule in Prometheus and routing in Alertmanager? core Model answer Alert rules live in Prometheus — they define when something is wrong (a PromQL condition held for a duration). Alertmanager decides what to do with those alerts: group rel","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","for"]},{"url":"slo/index.html","page":"SLO / SLI & alerting","section":"SLO / SLI & alerting.","tool":"slo","text":"SLO / SLI & alerting. Reliability is a product decision, not a technical one — and 100% is always the wrong target. This module teaches you to measure it precisely, budget it deliberately, and alert on it without pager fatigue.","keywords":["slo","sli","alerting"]},{"url":"slo/index.html#map","page":"SLO / SLI & alerting","section":"What's in this module","tool":"slo","text":"01 What's in this module SLOs turn \"the site is slow\" into a number — and that number drives everything from release velocity to on-call pages. Read in order: definitions first, then budgets, then alerting. SLI vs SLO vs SLA Precise definitions, worked examples, and what interviewers expect you to s","keywords":["what","in","this","module"]},{"url":"slo/sli-slo-sla.html","page":"SLI vs SLO vs SLA","section":"SLI vs SLO vs SLA.","tool":"slo","text":"SLI vs SLO vs SLA. Three terms that sound interchangeable but aren't. Get them precisely wrong in an interview and it signals you've never actually set reliability targets. Get them precisely right and you signal you've shipped with them.","keywords":["sli","vs","slo","sla"]},{"url":"slo/sli-slo-sla.html#definitions","page":"SLI vs SLO vs SLA","section":"The three terms, precisely","tool":"slo","text":"01 The three terms, precisely An SLI measures reality. An SLO is your internal target for that measure. An SLA is the external contract that penalises you if you breach a looser line. Each builds on the next. You can't set a target without a measure, and you can't sign a contract without a target to","keywords":["the","three","terms","precisely"]},{"url":"slo/sli-slo-sla.html#sli-craft","page":"SLI vs SLO vs SLA","section":"Crafting a good SLI","tool":"slo","text":"02 Crafting a good SLI A good SLI measures user-visible outcome , expressed as good events ÷ valid events . The formula forces you to define what \"good\" means and what requests are even in scope. It produces a 0–1 ratio that's comparable across windows. Availability SLI Successful HTTP responses (2x","keywords":["crafting","good","sli"]},{"url":"slo/sli-slo-sla.html#worked-example","page":"SLI vs SLO vs SLA","section":"A worked example","tool":"slo","text":"03 A worked example A checkout service. The team picks two SLIs from the user's perspective — availability and latency — and sets tighter internal targets than the external SLA. Layer Availability Latency (p99 <500 ms) SLI formula 2xx ÷ (2xx+5xx) requests <500 ms ÷ all requests SLO (internal) 99.9% ","keywords":["worked","example"]},{"url":"slo/sli-slo-sla.html#quiz-section","page":"SLI vs SLO vs SLA","section":"Check your model","tool":"slo","text":"04 Check your model Your team wants to SLI \"database connection pool utilisation < 70%\". What's wrong with this? The threshold is too low — it should be 90%. It measures a cause (internal resource), not a user-visible symptom. A user can't feel CPU utilisation. Nothing — internal metrics make fine S","keywords":["check","your","model"]},{"url":"slo/sli-slo-sla.html#interview","page":"SLI vs SLO vs SLA","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"slo","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What is the difference between an SLI, an SLO, and an SLA? core Model answer An SLI is a measured ratio: good events divided by valid events over a window — for example, the fraction of requests completing under 200 ms. An SLO is the internal target for that ratio — 99.9% ove","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"slo/error-budgets.html","page":"Error budgets & burn rate","section":"Error budgets & burn rate.","tool":"slo","text":"Error budgets & burn rate. The error budget converts your SLO from a reliability constraint into a shipping currency. When the budget is healthy you move fast; when it's nearly gone you stop and fix. It's the number that aligns product and engineering on the same terms.","keywords":["error","budgets","burn","rate"]},{"url":"slo/error-budgets.html#idea","page":"Error budgets & burn rate","section":"The idea","tool":"slo","text":"01 The idea Error budget = 100% − SLO — the allowed unreliability you may spend on deploys, experiments, and risk-taking. A 99.9% SLO means 0.1% of requests can fail — about 43 minutes of downtime in a 30-day window. That's real time and real request failures you've pre-authorised. The key insight: ","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"slo/error-budgets.html#burn-viz","page":"Error budgets & burn rate","section":"Watching the budget burn","tool":"slo","text":"02 Watching the budget burn The visualiser below simulates a 30-day error budget at 99.9% SLO. Drag the error rate slider to see how quickly the budget depletes and when exhaustion hits. 30-day error budget burndown (SLO = 99.9%) At 99.9% SLO, the monthly error budget is 43.2 minutes of allowed fail","keywords":["watching","the","budget","burn"]},{"url":"slo/error-budgets.html#policy","page":"Error budgets & burn rate","section":"The budget policy: fast vs frozen","tool":"slo","text":"03 The budget policy: fast vs frozen The budget's job is to make reliability a shared, numeric decision — not an ongoing argument between product and engineering. Budget healthy (>50%) Ship features aggressively Accept more deployment risk Run chaos experiments Invest in new infrastructure Budget ex","keywords":["the","budget","policy","fast","vs","frozen"]},{"url":"slo/error-budgets.html#quiz-section","page":"Error budgets & burn rate","section":"Check your model","tool":"slo","text":"04 Check your model A team's 99.9% SLO gives them 43 minutes of error budget per month. They've already used 40 minutes this month. Product wants to deploy a risky new feature. What should happen? Deploy anyway — 3 minutes is still some budget. Defer the risky deploy — the budget is nearly exhausted","keywords":["check","your","model"]},{"url":"slo/error-budgets.html#interview","page":"Error budgets & burn rate","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"slo","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What is an error budget and what does it unlock? core Model answer An error budget is 100% minus the SLO — the allowed unreliability for a window. For 99.9% over 30 days, that's 43 minutes of failures. The budget turns reliability into a shared, numeric decision: when it's he","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"slo/alerting-on-slos.html","page":"Multi-burn-rate alerting","section":"Multi-burn-rate alerting.","tool":"slo","text":"Multi-burn-rate alerting. Don't alert on CPU. Alert on how fast you're burning through user goodwill. Multi-burn-rate alerting — popularised by the Google SRE workbook — is the cleanest known way to get paged only when it matters, with enough signal to act.","keywords":["multi","burn","rate","alerting"]},{"url":"slo/alerting-on-slos.html#idea","page":"Multi-burn-rate alerting","section":"The idea: alert on rate, not threshold","tool":"slo","text":"01 The idea: alert on rate, not threshold Burn rate = how many multiples of the baseline error rate you're currently experiencing. A 1× burn spends the budget in exactly 30 days; a 14.4× burn spends it in ~2 days. The insight: a static CPU threshold fires indiscriminately — it has no idea whether us","keywords":["the","idea","alert","on","rate","not","threshold"]},{"url":"slo/alerting-on-slos.html#burn-viz","page":"Multi-burn-rate alerting","section":"Burn rate in action","tool":"slo","text":"02 Burn rate in action Drag the error rate slider to see your current burn rate multiple and which alert tier fires. Notice how even a small error rate spike can constitute a high burn if the SLO is tight. Multi-burn-rate alert tiers (SLO = 99.9%) At 99.9% SLO, baseline allowed error rate is 0.1%. A","keywords":["burn","rate","in","action"]},{"url":"slo/alerting-on-slos.html#multi-window","page":"Multi-burn-rate alerting","section":"Multi-window: killing false alarms","tool":"slo","text":"03 Multi-window: killing false alarms Pair a short window (fast detection) with a long window (confirmation) — alert only when both are elevated. This kills the two failure modes: missing slow burns and screaming at transient spikes. 1 Fast burn check (1h window) Burn rate >14.4×? A 5-minute spike o","keywords":["multi","window","killing","false","alarms"]},{"url":"slo/alerting-on-slos.html#quiz-section","page":"Multi-burn-rate alerting","section":"Check your model","tool":"slo","text":"04 Check your model Your SLO is 99.9%. You set a single alert: \"fire if error rate > 1% for 5 minutes.\" A real incident keeps error rate at 0.5% for 72 hours. What happens? The alert fires — 0.5% is a meaningful error rate. The alert never fires — 0.5% never crosses your threshold, even though 72 ho","keywords":["check","your","model"]},{"url":"slo/alerting-on-slos.html#interview","page":"Multi-burn-rate alerting","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"slo","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire What is burn rate and why alert on it instead of raw error rate? core Model answer Burn rate is your current error rate divided by the baseline error rate implied by your SLO. A 14.4× burn rate means you're consuming the monthly budget 14.4 times faster than planned — you'll ","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"interview/index.html","page":"How observability interviews work","section":"How observability interviews work.","tool":"interview","text":"How observability interviews work. Interviewers probe mental models, trade-offs, and how you walk through a real incident. They rarely ask trivia - they want to hear you reason from signal to root cause, out loud.","keywords":["how","observability","interviews","work"]},{"url":"interview/index.html#what-is-tested","page":"How observability interviews work","section":"What interviewers actually test","tool":"interview","text":"01 What interviewers actually test Three categories: conceptual trade-offs , incident walkthroughs , and design for observability . In plain English analogy Think of observability as a hospital triage system. Metrics are the vital-sign monitor that screams \"something is wrong.\" Traces are the MRI th","keywords":["what","interviewers","actually","test"]},{"url":"interview/index.html#signal-framework","page":"How observability interviews work","section":"The three-signal narration framework","tool":"interview","text":"02 The three-signal narration framework Metrics tell you SOMETHING is wrong → traces localize WHERE → logs explain WHY. Anchor every incident story to this progression. It shows you know the purpose of each signal, not just their definitions. Signal cascade metrics Something is wrong p99 SLO burn al","keywords":["the","three","signal","narration","framework"]},{"url":"interview/index.html#trade-offs","page":"How observability interviews work","section":"Core trade-offs to have ready","tool":"interview","text":"03 Core trade-offs to have ready These come up in every senior observability interview. Know the short answer for each. Push vs pull Pull (Prometheus scrape) = simpler auth + no retry storms. Push = works behind firewalls + ephemeral jobs. OTLP supports both. Head vs tail sampling Head sampling deci","keywords":["core","trade","offs","to","have","ready","service","checkout","status","500"]},{"url":"interview/index.html#star-method","page":"How observability interviews work","section":"How to tell an incident story (STAR)","tool":"interview","text":"04 How to tell an incident story (STAR) Use STAR to narrate incident walkthroughs — it keeps you focused and shows structured thinking. S Situation Set context: which service, what the SLO is, what the alert said. \"Our checkout p99 SLO is 500 ms; a multi-burn alert fired at 14× the 1-hour window.\" T","keywords":["how","to","tell","an","incident","story","star"]},{"url":"interview/index.html#map","page":"How observability interviews work","section":"Interview prep resources","tool":"interview","text":"05 Interview prep resources Work through these in order: drill the question bank, run the scenarios, then use cheatsheet + flashcards the night before. Question bank 30–40 drills across foundations, signals, standards, and reliability. Each with a model answer. Debugging scenarios Two full walkthrou","keywords":["interview","prep","resources"]},{"url":"interview/index.html#interview","page":"How observability interviews work","section":"Meta interview questions","tool":"interview","text":"06 Meta interview questions How would you add observability to a completely un-instrumented service? core Model answer Start with the boundaries: instrument HTTP in/out with the OTel SDK (auto-instrumentation often covers frameworks like Django or Express). That gets you traces + basic RED metrics f","keywords":["meta","interview","questions","trace_id"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html","page":"Observability question bank","section":"Observability question bank.","tool":"interview","text":"Observability question bank. 30+ questions grouped by theme. Each has a crisp model answer and a spoken version. Open the ones you're unsure about; close the ones you can recite cold.","keywords":["observability","question","bank"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#foundations","page":"Observability question bank","section":"Foundations","tool":"interview","text":"01 Foundations The warm-up layer — expect at least two of these early to gauge baseline understanding. What's the difference between observability and monitoring? warm-up Model answer Monitoring answers known questions: dashboards and thresholds on failure modes you predicted. Observability means yo","keywords":["foundations","trace_id","user_id","request_id"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#signals","page":"Observability question bank","section":"Signals — logs, metrics, traces","tool":"interview","text":"02 Signals — logs, metrics, traces What are the four metric types in Prometheus / OTel? core Model answer Counter: monotonically increasing (requests_total, errors_total) — only use rate() or increase() in PromQL. Gauge: up/down value (memory_bytes, queue_depth). Histogram: samples observations into","keywords":["signals","logs","metrics","traces","rate","increase","_bucket","_sum","_count","histogram_quantile","trace_id","service","checkout","AND","status","500","span_id","traceparent"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#standards","page":"Observability question bank","section":"Standards — OTel, Prometheus, PromQL","tool":"interview","text":"03 Standards — OTel, Prometheus, PromQL What are the three layers of OpenTelemetry? core Model answer API: vendor-neutral interfaces your application code calls — nothing ships to a backend. SDK: implements the API; handles sampling, context propagation, batching, export. Collector: a standalone age","keywords":["standards","otel","prometheus","promql","api","v1","otlp","metrics","rate","counter","irate","http","request","method","db","system","messaging","gen_ai","service","checkout","ERROR","span","status_code","500","duration","enable","feature","native","histograms"]},{"url":"interview/question-bank.html#reliability","page":"Observability question bank","section":"Reliability — SLOs, SLAs, alerting","tool":"interview","text":"04 Reliability — SLOs, SLAs, alerting What is the difference between SLI, SLO, and SLA? core Model answer SLI (Service Level Indicator) is the actual measurement — e.g. the ratio of successful requests. SLO (Service Level Objective) is the target you set internally — e.g. 99.9% of requests succeed. ","keywords":["reliability","slos","slas","alerting","attributes"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/index.html","page":"Debugging scenarios","section":"Debugging scenarios.","tool":"interview","text":"Debugging scenarios. Incident walkthroughs are where interviews are won or lost. These two scenarios teach the investigation method and give you a story to narrate, step by step.","keywords":["debugging","scenarios"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/index.html#method","page":"Debugging scenarios","section":"The investigation method","tool":"interview","text":"01 The investigation method Drive from metrics → traces → logs . Form a hypothesis, confirm with a signal, narrow — repeat until you have a root cause. In plain English analogy Think of it like diagnosing a car. The dashboard warning light (metric alert) tells you something is wrong. Plugging in a d","keywords":["the","investigation","method","trace_id"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/index.html#map","page":"Debugging scenarios","section":"The scenarios","tool":"interview","text":"02 The scenarios Each scenario starts with an alert and walks the full investigation. Practice narrating them out loud — that's the interview format. p99 latency spike SLO burn alert fires. Walk the trace waterfall to find a slow DB span on the critical path. Error-rate surge Fast burn-rate alert. C","keywords":["the","scenarios"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/latency-spike.html","page":"Scenario: p99 latency spike","section":"Scenario: p99 latency spike.","tool":"interview","text":"Scenario: p99 latency spike. Your multi-burn-rate alert fires at 2 a.m. p99 for POST /checkout has crossed the SLO threshold. Walk through the investigation exactly as you'd narrate it in an interview.","keywords":["scenario","p99","latency","spike"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/latency-spike.html#situation","page":"Scenario: p99 latency spike","section":"The situation","tool":"interview","text":"01 The situation SLO: p99 latency 500 ms for POST /checkout . The alert fired at a 14.4× burn rate on the 1-hour window. In plain English analogy 14.4× burn rate means at the current rate you'd exhaust the entire 30-day error budget in one hour. It's the equivalent of a fire alarm — something is bad","keywords":["the","situation","POST","checkout"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/latency-spike.html#investigation","page":"Scenario: p99 latency spike","section":"Step-by-step investigation","tool":"interview","text":"02 Step-by-step investigation 1 Confirm impact on the RED dashboard Open the Grafana RED panel for the checkout service. p99 latency is sitting at ~2 400 ms — nearly 5× the SLO threshold. Error rate is normal. Traffic is normal. This is a pure latency problem, not errors. 2 Click the exemplar — jump","keywords":["step","by","investigation","createOrder","INSERT","orders","trace_id","event","db_query_slow","table","duration_ms","query_type","plan","sequential_scan"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/latency-spike.html#trace","page":"Scenario: p99 latency spike","section":"The smoking-gun trace","tool":"interview","text":"03 The smoking-gun trace This is the trace that the exemplar linked to. The INSERT orders span is on the critical path — it directly determines the end-to-end latency of the checkout request. POST /checkout — 2 350 ms (p99 during incident) Trace waterfall: gateway span (2 350 ms) contains a Redis ca","keywords":["the","smoking","gun","trace","INSERT","orders"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/latency-spike.html#narration","page":"Scenario: p99 latency spike","section":"How to narrate this in an interview","tool":"interview","text":"04 How to narrate this in an interview Use the STAR structure. Keep it under 3 minutes — the interviewer will probe deeper where they want detail. STAR What to say S ituation \"Our checkout p99 SLO is 500 ms. A 14× multi-burn alert fired at 2 a.m.\" T ask \"Confirm user impact, find the root cause, res","keywords":["how","to","narrate","this","in","an","interview"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/latency-spike.html#interview","page":"Scenario: p99 latency spike","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"interview","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire How did you get from the metric to the trace without manual searching? core Model answer Via a histogram exemplar — a data point on the histogram that carries a trace_id from a real request in that latency bucket. Clicking it in Grafana opens the exact trace in Tempo. This is","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire","trace_id"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/error-surge.html","page":"Scenario: error-rate surge","section":"Scenario: error-rate surge.","tool":"interview","text":"Scenario: error-rate surge. A fast burn-rate alert fires at 14× on the 1-hour window. The checkout service is returning 5xx errors at a rate that would exhaust the monthly error budget in minutes.","keywords":["scenario","error","rate","surge"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/error-surge.html#situation","page":"Scenario: error-rate surge","section":"The situation","tool":"interview","text":"01 The situation SLO: 99.9% of requests succeed. Alert fired: 14.4× burn rate, fast window. Error budget burning fast. In plain English analogy The burn-rate alert is like a fuel gauge dropping to empty in real-time instead of gradually. At 1× burn you'd empty the tank in 30 days. At 14.4× you'd emp","keywords":["the","situation"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/error-surge.html#burn-widget","page":"Scenario: error-rate surge","section":"The burn-rate indicator","tool":"interview","text":"02 The burn-rate indicator Interact with the slider to see how a 4.5% error rate maps to a burn rate multiple — and which alert tier it triggers. Error-rate → burn rate → alert tier At a 4.5% error rate against a 99.9% SLO: burn rate = 4.5 / 0.1 = 45×. Well above the 14.4× page threshold. The monthl","keywords":["the","burn","rate","indicator"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/error-surge.html#investigation","page":"Scenario: error-rate surge","section":"Step-by-step investigation","tool":"interview","text":"03 Step-by-step investigation 1 Fast burn alert fires → RED dashboard Open the Grafana RED panel. Error rate for POST /checkout is 4.5% — started ~12 minutes ago. Latency is normal. Traffic is normal. This is a clean error spike, not a latency problem. 2 Filter by endpoint and version Break down the","keywords":["step","by","investigation","POST","checkout","http","route","v2","createOrder","ERROR","payments","service","UNAVAILABLE","trace_id","event","payments_call_failed","reason","connection","refused","endpoint","svc","abc123"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/error-surge.html#narration","page":"Scenario: error-rate surge","section":"Narrate it in an interview (STAR)","tool":"interview","text":"04 Narrate it in an interview (STAR) STAR What to say S ituation \"99.9% availability SLO for checkout. A 14× burn-rate alert fired on the fast window, 12 minutes after a deploy.\" T ask \"Identify which endpoint and version. Find the root cause fast — the budget was burning at 45×.\" A ction \"RED error","keywords":["narrate","it","in","an","interview","star"]},{"url":"interview/scenarios/error-surge.html#interview","page":"Scenario: error-rate surge","section":"Interview rapid-fire","tool":"interview","text":"05 Interview rapid-fire Why use a multi-window burn-rate alert instead of a simple error-rate threshold? core Model answer A single error-rate threshold alerts on spikes that might be transient — a 5-second burst triggers the page even if it self-resolves. Multi-window burn rates (e.g. 14.4× on 1-ho","keywords":["interview","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"interview/cheatsheet.html","page":"Observability cheat-sheet","section":"Observability cheat-sheet.","tool":"interview","text":"Observability cheat-sheet. Night-before rapid reference. One screen of the calls interviewers make you make — skim, recall, walk in calm.","keywords":["observability","cheat","sheet"]},{"url":"interview/cheatsheet.html#sheets","page":"Observability cheat-sheet","section":"The reference cards","tool":"interview","text":"01 The reference cards Each card answers one \"when do I pick which?\" in a glance. If you can reproduce these from memory, you're ready. The 3 pillars — when to use each Signal Use when Best for Weakness Metrics Alerting, SLOs, dashboards Aggregated health; cheap at scale No per-request context; card","keywords":["the","reference","cards","rate","increase","http_requests_total","min_over_time","memory_usage_bytes","histogram_quantile","request_duration_seconds","status","request_duration_seconds_bucket","errors","requests","001","avg_over_time","queue_depth"]},{"url":"interview/flashcards.html","page":"Observability flashcards","section":"Observability flashcards.","tool":"interview","text":"Observability flashcards. Active recall beats re-reading. Flip a card, answer out loud first, then mark it - Got it or Review . Shuffle pushes your gaps to the front. Progress saves locally.","keywords":["observability","flashcards"]},{"url":"interview/flashcards.html#decks","page":"Observability flashcards","section":"decks","tool":"interview","text":"Observability 0 / 0 known Shuffle Reset What is cardinality in the context of metrics? tap to flip The total number of unique time series a metric generates — the product of all unique label-value combinations. High cardinality (e.g. adding user_id as a label) can OOM Prometheus. Got it Review What ","keywords":["decks","service","checkout","ERROR","json","duration","span","http","status_code","500"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html","page":"Glossary","section":"Glossary.","tool":"home","text":"Glossary. A–Z definitions for every term in this guide. One line each — crisp and precise. Use Ctrl/Cmd+F to jump directly to a term.","keywords":["glossary"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#glossary-az","page":"Glossary","section":"A–Z","tool":"home","text":"01 A–Z Alertmanager Prometheus component that receives firing alerts, deduplicates them, groups by label, silences, and routes to receivers (PagerDuty, Slack, email). Burn rate How fast error budget is being consumed relative to sustainable pace; 1× = on budget, 14.4× = page threshold for the 1-hour","keywords":["traceparent","rate","increase","SLO","window","node_exporter","postgres_exporter","metrics","_bucket","_sum","_count","histogram_quantile","service","checkout","ERROR","json","enable","feature","native","histograms","sum","by","span","http","status_code","500","duration"]}];
